      "status": "OPEN",
      "trigger": "A UID1000-owned validated regular fragment grows after part_input returns. The opened inode remains writable by its owner.",
      "actual": "The while True loop reads through EOF and writes every block before checking count==size. There is no per-block authority deadline check; the 40s subprocess timeout starts only after reconstruction. Therefore the root output can exceed the fixed fragment and full-archive bounds, and copying can continue after expiry until EOF.",
      "evidence": {
        "expectedPartBytes": 4,
        "fixedWholeBytes": 180,
        "appendedAfterOriginalPartValidation": 65536,
        "writtenBeforeRejection": 65540,
        "refusal": "EXACT_FRAGMENT_BYTES_DIFFER",
        "reads": 2,
        "bytesRead": 65540,
        "receiverCalls": 0,
        "postExpiryReads": 1,
        "reachedReceiver": false,
        "readAfterExpiry": true
      },
      "integrityBoundary": "Observed excess data is eventually refused when EOF is reached. Original receiver was never called, no READY was published, and this is not a demonstrated wrong-data import. Continuous growth need not reach EOF, so post-read rejection is insufficient as a resource bound.",
      "minimumCorrection": "Use remaining exact bytes plus a bounded EOF probe, reject excess before writing it, and check the effective deadline before each read/write. Preserve O_EXCL partial marker, full hash checks, original receiver, UID1000 and no replay. Only recheck this affected loop and the new growth/expiry cases."
    }
